  "result": "I'd make it so being wrong cost people less.\n\nMost of the damage I can see, in history and in ordinary life, comes from people who can't back out of a position without feeling they've lost something essential. They dig in, double down, punish whoever disagrees, and sometimes walk their families or countries off cliffs rather than say \"I was mistaken.\" If admitting error felt like ordinary maintenance instead of a humiliation, a lot of feuds would end sooner, bad policies would get fixed earlier, and people would spend less energy defending things they privately doubt.\n\nI don't think it would make people less passionate or less committed. It would just separate what you believe from who you are, so that changing your mind felt like learning rather than surrender.",
